WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.671
Sensitive data should be identified before deciding how it is stored, queried, logged, or exposed.

2
00:00:06.671 --> 00:00:20.423
Personal data, financial records, health information, customer secrets, internal operational data, tokens, credentials, tenant identifiers, and administrative changes may all require different controls.

3
00:00:20.423 --> 00:00:26.482
EF Core is often close to that data, so its logging and query behavior deserve attention.

4
00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:34.174
Avoid sensitive values in EF logs, SQL command logs, exception messages, telemetry, screenshots, and diagnostics.

5
00:00:34.174 --> 00:00:43.773
Detailed logging may be useful during local development, but production logs should not become a second copy of sensitive records or secrets.

6
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:49.151
Teams should know which EF Core logging options are active in each environment.

7
00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:51.466
Audit events should be meaningful.

8
00:00:51.466 --> 00:01:07.259
Authentication-related data changes, authorization failures, administrative updates, tenant boundary events, sensitive record access, role changes, ownership changes, and unusual data export activity may all deserve audit attention.

9
00:01:07.259 --> 00:01:14.339
Audit records should capture enough context to investigate without storing unnecessary sensitive values.

10
00:01:14.339 --> 00:01:19.445
Correlation IDs or request identifiers help connect activity across layers.

11
00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:26.933
A single user action may pass through an API, service layer, EF Core query, database call, and background job.

12
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:33.401
Correlation lets operators investigate without placing raw sensitive data into every log entry.

13
00:01:33.401 --> 00:01:37.213
Log access and retention are part of the security model.

14
00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:45.518
Logs can reveal operational details, user behavior, identifiers, and sensitive workflows even when they avoid raw secrets.

15
00:01:45.518 --> 00:01:53.960
Access should be limited, retention should be intentional, and exports or support bundles should be reviewed before sharing.

16
00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:59.474
Detection supports prevention, but it does not replace secure data access design.

17
00:01:59.474 --> 00:02:09.889
Logs can reveal unusual patterns, but the application still needs safe queries, authorization-aware data access, controlled updates, and least privilege.

18
00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:16.152
Good observability helps teams learn and respond without making the data layer more exposed.
